      "q": "39. If $\\int\\limits_{0}^{a}{\\frac{1}{1+{{x}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}}$ then what is value of a.",
      "a": "$\\begin{align}\n\n  & \\int{\\frac{1}{1+{{x}^{2}}}=}{{\\tan }^{-1}}x+C \\\\ \n\n & \\int\\limits_{0}^{a}{\\frac{1}{1+{{x}^{2}}}={{\\tan }^{-1}}}\\left( a \\right) \\\\ \n\n & {{\\tan }^{-1}}\\left( a \\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \n\n & \\tan \\frac{\\pi }{4}=a \\\\ \n\n & \\Rightarrow a=1 \\\\ \n\n\\end{align}$\n40. $\\int\\limits_{a}^{b}{f\\left( x \\right)dx+\\int\\limits_{b}^{a}{f\\left( x \\right)}dx}$\nAns:\nUsing the property $\\int\\limits_{p}^{q}{f\\left( x \\right)}dx=-\\int\\limits_{q}^{p}{f\\left( x \\right)}dx$ we have,\n$\\begin{align}\n\n  & \\int\\limits_{a}^{b}{f\\left( x \\right)}dx+\\int\\limits_{b}^{a}{f\\left( x \\right)}dx=\\int\\limits_{a}^{b}{f\\left( x \\right)}dx-\\int\\limits_{a}^{b}{f\\left( x \\right)}dx \\\\ \n\n & =0 \\\\ \n\n\\end{align}$\n4 Mark Questions"
